  "chapter_notes": {
    "summary": "James 2 in Hebrew is saturated with Torah vocabulary and direct OT quotations. The most striking feature is beit din (house of judgment, v2) where Greek has synagogue -- no back-translator from synagogue would produce a rabbinic court term. The chapter quotes Leviticus 19:18 verbatim (v8), uses torah directly alongside dat to distinguish instruction from binding decree (v8), and quotes Genesis 15:6 verbatim (v22). The dat besimchah (law in joy, v12) continues the joy-law theme from chapter 1. Faith without works cannot lekadesh (make holy, v14) -- not save as in Greek. The setanim (adversaries, PLURAL, v18) differs from Greek's demons. The Akeidah (binding of Isaac) uses korban (sacrifice) and mizbeach (altar) -- Temple vocabulary. Rahab's story uses meraglim (spies) and vatitmenem (she hid them), drawing directly from Joshua 2."
  }
